A||N||4||whamel||Wheels and rims||||1||
Z||000000||whamel||09-12-2000||10:21 AM||||After looking at all the cool wheels this past weekend at Fiero-rama, I am really wanting to upgrade the wheels in my Fiero.  What is the offset and stats to order aftermarket rims?  I'd really like to upgrade to 16 inch rims and tires while I'm at it.  <BR>Thanks,<BR>Wade||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierocrazy||09-12-2000||11:05 AM||||I am not sure about about 16 inch wheels, i would think you could get more offset on the rear wheel,( rim would be higher and can clear the toe knuckle better) I cant imagine why you would want wider than a 205 on the front.  Factory is 100mm bolt pattern with a 30mm offset, I would stick to that and get 7 inch rims, remember fieros are manual stering, and 16 inch tires are big bucks if you go with an 8 inch rim<P>85GT 3.2||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||09-12-2000||11:14 AM||||i have 16x7.5 35mm offset rims on my '87 coupe with 225/50-16 on the front. i don't think steering effort is much harder. i have been recently playing with an '84 coupe that has the stock 14" rims and tires on it and can feel no real difference in steering effort.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eric Marsh||09-12-2000||11:43 PM||||I've got a 17 by 7 on front with a 45 offset and an 18 by 8 on the rear with a 40 offset.<P>Eric<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||maninblack||09-13-2000||08:52 AM||||just make sure they are hubcentric or a set of hubcentric rings are avliable for your fiero.otherwise it would not fit properly <BR>and you would encounter shaking at high<BR>speed||||reg||3
